-- title:
--   A short walk realizing connectivity through a finite family of blocks
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a configuration on $\mathbb{R}^d$ and let $W$ be a *cone choice*, i.e. an assignment of one double cone to each subset ("block") of $\mathbb{R}^d$. Two blocks $Q \ne P$ are adjacent in the associated *choice graph* when the chosen cone of one of them, based at each of its points, contains all points of the other; this adjacency is symmetric and irreflexive, so it defines a simple graph on blocks.
--
--   Let $T$ be a **finite** family of blocks, let $Q \in T$, and suppose $Q$ and $P$ are connected *through $T$*, meaning that $P$ is reachable from $Q$ by the reflexive-transitive closure of the relation "both endpoints lie in $T$ and are adjacent in the choice graph". Then there is an actual walk realizing this, staying in $T$ and of length shorter than the size of $T$:
--
--   $$\exists\, w : Q \rightsquigarrow P \text{ in the choice graph, with } \operatorname{supp}(w) \subseteq T \ \text{ and } \ \operatorname{length}(w) < \#T .$$
--
--   The value of the statement is the passage from an abstract reachability relation, which records only *that* a chain exists, to a concrete walk carrying a length and a list of edges — the form needed whenever one must subsequently count edges or measure them. The length bound $<\#T$ is the usual "no repeated vertex" economy, obtained here for free from finiteness of $T$.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** Reachability is `Relation.ReflTransGen` on the relation "$Q \in T$ and $P \in T$ and choice-adjacent", the walk is a Mathlib `SimpleGraph.Walk`, and $\#T$ is `Set.ncard`; finiteness of $T$ is an explicit hypothesis, so `ncard` is the true cardinality. The hypothesis $Q \in T$ is needed to start the walk inside $T$ in the reflexive case $P = Q$.
